Introduction

Are you passionate about protecting public health and ensuring safe, reliable drinking water for communities across Connecticut?

Join our team as an Environmental Analyst 3 and play a key role in advancing statewide implementation of drinking water regulations that safeguard public health.

The Connecticut Department of Public Health (DPH) is seeking a motivated and knowledgeable Environmental Analyst 3
to serve as the lead analyst in the Lead and Copper Rule Implementation Unit (LCRIU) within the Drinking Water Section. This position will support statewide efforts to reduce lead exposure and ensure compliance with the Lead and Copper Rule (LCR), Lead and Copper Rule Revisions (LCRR), and the upcoming Lead and Copper Rule Improvements (LCRI).

In this leadership role, you will guide lower-level analysts, oversee regulatory implementation, and ensure consistency in how Connecticut’s public water systems monitor, report, and reduce lead and copper levels.

Highlights

Location: Hartford, CT
Schedule: Monday - Friday
Hours/Shift: Full-time (35 hours per week), First Shift
Please note: A f lex and/or teleworking schedule may be permitted

What We Can Offer You

Visit our new State Employee Benefits Overview page!
Professional growth and development opportunities
A health work/life balance to all employees

i n This Role You Will

Lead and oversee the work of lower level environmental analysts in the LCRIU.
Ensure consistent implementation of current and new lead and copper rules statewide.
Develop and provide materials to train DWS staff, public water systems, and certified operators on current and new lead and copper rules.
Generate lead and copper rule compliance reports to create candidate violations and review calculations to determine lead and copper action level exceedances. Oversee LCRIU staff review and actions taken on all candidate violations and exceedances.
Update and maintain public water system information in DWS databases and tracking applications.
Oversee the designation of optimal water quality parameter ranges for corrosion control treatment systems.
Review water quality data to determine compliance with corrosion control treatment technique requirements.
Review and process monthly operation reports for corrosion control treatment systems.
Review and process lead and copper monitoring reduction requests for public water systems.
Review and process requests from public water systems to deem to have optimized corrosion control treatment.
Review public water system sampling site plans and verify compliance with lead and copper sample site selection requirements.
Develop new forms, operating procedures, and guidance for internal and external use necessary to implement the current and new lead and copper rules.
Provide regulatory and technical assistance to staff, public water systems, water system operators, consultants, and local health on issues related to lead and copper.

More About The Agency

The Mission of the Department of Public Health (DPH) is to protect and improve the health and safety of the people of Connecticut by:

Assuring the conditions in which people can be healthy
Preventing disease, injury, and disability
Promoting the equal enjoyment of the highest attainable standard of health, which is a human right and a priority of the state.

Selection Plan

For Assistance In Applying

Check out our Applicant Tips on How to Apply ! Need more resources? Visit our Applicant Toolkit for additional support throughout the recruitment process.

Before You Apply

Meet Minimum Qualifications: Ensure you meet the Minimum Qualifications listed on the job opening by the job closing date. You must specify your qualifications on your application. The minimum experience and training requirements listed must be met by the close date on the job opening, unless otherwise specified.
Educational Credits: List your earned credits and degrees from accredited institutions accurately on your application. To receive educational credits towards qualification, the institution must be accredited. If the institution of higher learning is located outside of the U.S., you are responsible for providing documentation from a recognized USA accrediting service which specializes in determining foreign education equivalencies to the contact listed below by the closing date listed on the job posting.
Resume Policy: Per Public Act 21-69 , resumes are not accepted during the initial application process. As the recruitment process progresses, candidates may be required to submit additional documentation to support their qualification(s) for this position. This documentation may include: a cover letter, resume, transcripts, diplomas, performance reviews, attendance records, supervisory references, licensure, etc., at the request and discretion of the hiring agency.
Preferred Shift/Location: Select all location(s) and shift(s) you are willing to work on your application. Failure to do so may result in not being considered for vacancies in that specific location or shift.
Timely Submission: All application materials must be received by the job posting deadline. You will be unable to make revisions once you officially submit your application to the State. Late submissions are rarely accepted, with exceptions only for documented events that incapacitate individuals during the entire duration of the job posting. Request exceptions by emailing DAS.SHRM@ct.gov .
Salary Calculations: For current state employees, salary calculations are not necessarily comparable from one of the three branches of state government (i.e., Executive, Legislative, Judicial) to the other.
Note: The only way to apply to this posting is via the ‘Apply’ or ‘Apply Online’ buttons on the official State of Connecticut Online Employment Center job posting.

After You Apply

